+# Managed gateway contracts
+
+These optional interfaces support applications that own a gateway and supply
+their own command logic. They do not add a receiver, model tool, cron job, outbox,
+or credential store. Application authorization and durable operation deduplication
+remain the consumer's responsibility.
+
+## Authenticated command context
+
+`PluginContext.register_command(..., with_context=True)` invokes a sync or async
+`handler(raw_args: str, context: TrustedCommandContext)`. The frozen dataclass in
+`gateway.managed_contracts` has these fields:
+
+| Field | Source |
+| --- | --- |
+| `platform` | Ingress `SessionSource.platform.value` |
+| `profile` | Routed source profile, or the runner's primary profile |
+| `transport_profile` | Profile owning the receiving adapter; may differ from the routed profile |
+| `user_id`, `chat_id`, `thread_id`, `chat_type` | Ingress `SessionSource` |
+| `source_message_id` | `native_source_message_id(event)` before hooks; may be `None` |
+
+The runner snapshots the source before `pre_gateway_dispatch`, restores its
+identity after the hook, authorizes it through the existing gateway policy, then
+creates this context. The pre-dispatch hook can rewrite text, skip the message,
+or stage an unapproved pairing request. It cannot authorize a different source or
+invent a message ID. Consumer commands must not accept authority from `raw_args`.
+If a write requires a source message ID, the consumer must reject a missing one.
+
+`native_source_message_id(event)` is also available to an unapproved pairing
+observer, but does not authorize the observer. It excludes events whose adapter
+sets `MessageEvent.message_id_is_synthetic=True`. WeCom sets that flag when
+`body.msgid` is absent: callback request IDs and generated UUIDs remain internal
+bookkeeping IDs. They cannot serve as message identity for pairing or writes.
+Other adapters must also set this flag when they generate fallback IDs.
+
+Internal events never receive this context. Without the opt-in, existing
+`handler(raw_args)` callbacks keep their signature. Contextual callbacks called
+outside authenticated gateway dispatch fail explicitly. A plugin exception is a
+handled command error and never becomes model input. Returning `None` or `""`
+is also handled, so a callback that sends its own response does not trigger an
+additional automatic reply.
+
+This is a transport provenance contract for reviewed in-process plugins, not an
+OS sandbox against arbitrary Python code installed by the operator.
+
+## Strict delivery
+
+```python
+from gateway.managed_contracts import StrictTarget, send_strict
+
+receipt = await send_strict(
+    runner,
+    StrictTarget(context.transport_profile, context.platform,
+                 context.chat_id, context.thread_id),
+    "Operation completed.",
+    timeout=30.0,
+)
+```
+
+`strict_send_supported(platform)` and `STRICT_SEND_PLATFORMS` describe implemented
+adapter capabilities. They do not prove account connectivity. The sender requires
+the exact profile's connected adapter and never connects, selects a home chat,
+changes a target, splits a payload, or retries. Other adapters default to
+`strict_delivery_unsupported`. This entry point supports text only and does not
+interpret Hermes attachment directives or read local files.
+
+The returned dict has `status`, `message_id`, `receipt_id`, `requested_target`,
+`target`, and `error_code`. `target` is the actual confirmed target, or `None` if
+unconfirmed. `requested_target` always preserves the explicit request.
+
+- `sent`: one complete request has a matching platform receipt and target.
+- `not_sent`: preflight prevented a request, or the platform explicitly rejected it.
+- `unknown`: acceptance cannot be established, including timeouts, connection
+  errors, incomplete receipts, unexpected target changes, and partial delivery.
+
+Persist `unknown` and require reconciliation or an explicit resend decision. A
+cancelled coroutine does not prove the platform did not receive a request. This
+API does not claim exactly-once delivery across process crashes.
+
+Telegram sends one plain-text request, bounded by 4096 UTF-16 units. It requires
+numeric chat IDs and optional numeric thread IDs. A rejected thread is never
+downgraded to the root. The forum General topic is explicit thread `"1"`;
+unconfirmed routing returns `unknown`. Native `direct_messages_topic_id` routing
+is not supported by this contract.
+
+WeCom supports private chats only, bounded conservatively by 4000 UTF-8 bytes.
+It selects one proactive request with a fresh `req_id` before any send; passive
+reply IDs are reused by other stream/control frames and cannot identify this
+request uniquely. Known groups return `strict_group_unsupported`. A successful
+WeCom ACK may omit the actual message ID: `message_id` then remains `None`, while
+`receipt_id` is the correlated request ID. An ACK timeout never triggers a passive
+or proactive retry. These are transport ACKs, not recipient read receipts.
+
+Adapters expose `send_strict(chat_id, content, *, thread_id=None)` as an optional
+capability. Legacy `send(..., metadata={"strict_target": True, ...})` also enters
+the strict path on Telegram and WeCom; normal callers retain existing behavior.
+
+## Integration-only gateway
+
+```yaml
+gateway:
+  integration_only: true
+```
+
+The default remains `false`. When enabled, the gateway retains adapter intake,
+existing authorization/pairing, authenticated plugin commands, and native
+`/help`, `/status`, `/whoami`, `/platforms`, `/version`, `/start`. Other native
+commands, exec quick commands, skill-to-model rewriting, ordinary chat model
+fallback, and internal synthetic turns are disabled. The agent entry point also
+rejects execution before initializing a model or proxy.
+
+Each inbound event is dispatched as a tracked background command task, without
+agent-session follow-up merging or text batching. Concurrent commands keep their
+own payload and native message ID; the transport reader remains free to receive
+delivery ACKs. Consumers should bound concurrent command work as appropriate.
+
+Startup skips turn prewarming, process/session recovery, boot delivery replay,
+automatic agent watchers, hosted room workers, MCP discovery, cron scheduling and
+delivery draining, housekeeping, provider auth keepalive, and tool auto-install
+checks. Connection recovery, loop heartbeat, and lifecycle/drain control remain.
+Reconnecting does not replay old delivery obligations or resume agents.
+
+Use a dedicated profile with only the intended platform and reviewed consumer
+plugin enabled. Arbitrary plugin hook code remains trusted Python; this mode is
+not a sandbox for a plugin that directly starts its own model or process.
+
+The focused `tests/gateway/test_managed_contracts.py` suite exercises actual plugin
+discovery, gateway authorization/dispatch, adapters and request correlation with
+synthetic transport replies in temporary homes. It is local behavior evidence,
+not proof of delivery through a configured real account.
